#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""weekly-backup - run backup-sensitive on a clock, on a day with no session.

The gap this closes. scripts/backup-sensitive is trustworthy (encrypt-then-MAC, plus a
decrypt-and-list restore test that gates the marker write), but a trustworthy backup that
nobody runs is not a backup. Every other channel in this repo (horizon at SessionStart,
repo-doctor on commit, /weekly) needs Sam to be sitting there. Left to those, the off-site
archive drifts past horizon's 21-day tripwire and the tripwire only fires when a session
happens to open, which is the hazard `backup-unproven` in ai/ERRORS.md. This is the only
thing here that runs without the operator present.

Shape. A launchd user agent (com.northwindlabs.weekly-backup, source of truth in
scripts/com.northwindlabs.weekly-backup.plist) fires this once a week. The run is gated on
the marker's own date token rather than on the schedule, which makes it idempotent and safe
to fire late. Late is the normal case: launchd runs a missed StartCalendarInterval on the
next wake, so a laptop asleep at the scheduled minute still gets its backup.

The plist is the SOURCE OF TRUTH and is versioned. launchd only ever loads from
~/Library/LaunchAgents, so the installed copy lives there, but a job that exists only in
~/Library/LaunchAgents is an unversioned config that dies with the laptop and leaves no
audit trail. __REPO__ is a placeholder the install command substitutes, which keeps the
committed file machine-independent.

================================================================================
INSTALL. Three steps, and STEP 2 IS NOT OPTIONAL.
================================================================================

1. Register the agent:

     R=$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)
     chmod +x "$R/scripts/weekly-backup"
     mkdir -p "$R/outputs/ops/backup-runs"
     sed "s|__REPO__|$R|g" "$R/scripts/com.northwindlabs.weekly-backup.plist" \
       > ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.northwindlabs.weekly-backup.plist
     launchctl bootstrap gui/$(id -u) \
       ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.northwindlabs.weekly-backup.plist

2. GRANT FULL DISK ACCESS TO /Library/Developer/CommandLineTools/usr/bin/python3
   (the exact path matters, see below). macOS protects ~/Desktop, ~/Documents,
   ~/Downloads and iCloud Drive with TCC. If this repo sits in any of them, a launchd
   agent is not the terminal and inherits none of the terminal's grants, so without this
   the job dies at exit 2 with "Operation not permitted" before a single line of Python
   runs. Observed failure shape: the agent could list $HOME and read ~/Library fine, and
   was refused on the repo directory with errno 1. A background agent gets no permission
   dialog, it is simply denied. So this has to be granted by hand and it has to be granted
   first. Grant it even if the repo currently sits somewhere unprotected: it costs nothing
   and it survives the day someone moves the repo.

     System Settings, Privacy and Security, Full Disk Access, click +,
     press Cmd Shift G, paste
       /Library/Developer/CommandLineTools/usr/bin/python3, add it, leave the toggle on.

   The grant target must be the Command Line Tools path above, not /usr/bin/python3.
   /usr/bin/python3 is a SHIM, not an interpreter. It execs whatever xcode-select points
   at, typically /Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer/usr/bin/python3, so the process
   TCC judges is Xcode's binary and a grant on the shim path does not cover it. The launchd
   log records the exact failure in that case: "Xcode.app/.../python3: can't open file
   .../scripts/weekly-backup: [Errno 1] Operation not permitted".

   The Command Line Tools path is pinned because it is Apple-signed, concrete rather than a
   shim, independent of xcode-select and of whether Xcode is installed at all, and still
   3.9.x, which is why both scripts are kept 3.9-clean. It resolves to
   .../CommandLineTools/Library/Frameworks/Python3.framework/Versions/3.9/bin/python3.9;
   the GUI grant follows the symlink for you.

3. Force one real run, WITH SOMEONE AT THE KEYBOARD:

     launchctl kickstart -k gui/$(id -u)/com.northwindlabs.weekly-backup
     cat "$R/outputs/ops/backup-runs/$(date +%F).log"

   Expect one interactive moment. The first time launchd rather than a terminal runs
   `security find-generic-password`, macOS may show a keychain dialog. Click Always
   Allow once. Do it here, not at 06:20 on a Sunday with nobody there to click, or the
   job hangs until its one hour timeout. If no dialog appears, nothing was needed.
   The marker is normally fresh, so this run will report a skip. To prove the whole
   cycle in the launchd context, run the same command after temporarily adding
   <string>--force</string> to ProgramArguments, then take it out again.

UNINSTALL:

     launchctl bootout gui/$(id -u)/com.northwindlabs.weekly-backup
     rm ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.northwindlabs.weekly-backup.plist

================================================================================
Why the ProgramArguments are what they are
================================================================================

The job runs a five line Python bootstrap rather than the script directly. Two reasons.
It keeps the TCC identity on /Library/Developer/CommandLineTools/usr/bin/python3, so the
grant stays narrow (a /bin/sh -c wrapper would have moved the grant onto /bin/sh, which
every shell script on the machine would then inherit). And it gives the one failure that
scripts/weekly-backup cannot report a voice: if the script cannot be read at all, the script
obviously cannot notify, log, or advance anything, and the run would be silent apart from a
line in launchd.log that nobody opens. The bootstrap itself touches nothing inside the repo,
so it always runs, and it turns "could not even start" into a banner. Exit 0 and exit 1 are
the script's own outcomes and it stays quiet for those; the script handles its own noise.

The bootstrap is column zero on purpose. Indenting it is an IndentationError that
plutil cannot see.

-I (isolated mode) is load bearing, not decoration. `python3 -c` puts the working
directory on sys.path, the working directory here is the repo, and if the repo is
TCC-protected then without -I even `import subprocess` dies with errno 1 before the
bootstrap can say anything. Observed: without -I the job exited 1 with an import traceback
and no banner; with -I it reaches the notification. -I also implies -E and -s, which a
scheduled job wants anyway.

Other choices:
- /opt/homebrew/bin first in PATH: rclone usually exists only there, and putting it first
  makes the openssl binary deterministic across interactive and scheduled runs.
- Sunday 06:20: weekends are off-days (daily-log hard rule 6), so no session is scheduled
  against it. If the laptop is asleep, launchd runs the missed interval on the next wake
  and the marker gate still lets it through, so sleep can delay the backup but never
  skip it.
- Background plus low priority IO: because launchd defers a missed run to wake, a
  collision with a working session is always possible, so the run is throttled rather
  than scheduled around.
- RunAtLoad false: installing or logging in must not push a full archive to the remote.

Not a digest. A daily-digest job was considered and cut: horizon already runs at every
SessionStart, and a dated file nobody opens is a second delivery channel for a signal that
is already delivered. What survives is the backup, which is the half with a hazard behind it.

Usage:
  scripts/weekly-backup           back up if the marker is BACKUP_EVERY_DAYS+ days old
  scripts/weekly-backup --force   back up regardless of the marker's age
  scripts/weekly-backup --quiet   no desktop notification on failure
  scripts/weekly-backup --help    print this text and exit

NOT ON macOS (Linux, WSL). launchd, the plist, Full Disk Access and the Keychain do not
apply; the script itself is portable. Give backup-sensitive its passphrase through
OPERATOR_OS_BACKUP_PASSPHRASE_FILE (see that script's PASSPHRASE STORE paragraph) and
schedule this with cron instead, Sundays 06:20 local:

     R=$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)
     ( crontab -l 2>/dev/null; echo "20 6 * * 0 cd $R && OPERATOR_OS_BACKUP_PASSPHRASE_FILE=$HOME/.config/operator-os/backup-pass python3 scripts/weekly-backup --quiet >> $R/outputs/ops/backup-runs/cron.log 2>&1" ) | crontab -

The failure notification is osascript and is skipped off macOS; the dated log and the
un-advanced marker remain the channels that cannot be suppressed.

Exit 0 when the backup ran clean, or when it was correctly skipped (marker still fresh, or
another backup-sensitive run holds the lock). Exit 1 on failure, and failure is loud three
ways: a desktop notification, a dated log under outputs/ops/backup-runs/, and the marker's
date token not advancing, which horizon nags on at 21 days.

Read-only on the repo. The only things this writes are its own gitignored log directory and,
through backup-sensitive, the gitignored .backup-marker and the remote object. It commits
nothing, sends nothing, spends nothing.
"""
import os
import pwd
import subprocess
import sys
import time
from datetime import date, datetime, timedelta
from pathlib import Path

HERE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent
ROOT = Path(subprocess.run(["git", "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel"], cwd=str(HERE),
                           capture_output=True, text=True).stdout.strip() or str(HERE.parent))
MARKER = ROOT / ".backup-marker"
LOG_DIR = ROOT / "outputs" / "ops" / "backup-runs"
BACKUP_EVERY_DAYS = 7
BACKUP_TIMEOUT_S = 3600
KEEP_DAYS = 180
LOCK_BUSY_RC = 3          # backup-sensitive's "another run holds the lock" exit code
TIMEOUT_RC = 124
SPAWN_FAIL_RC = 127


def marker_text() -> str:
    try:
        return MARKER.read_text(errors="ignore").strip()
    except OSError:
        return ""


def marker_age(today: date):
    """Age in days from the marker's own date token, or None if absent or unparseable.

    The date token, not the file mtime: a touched file is not a proven archive. This is the
    same field horizon reads, so the gate here and the nag there can never disagree.
    """
    body = marker_text()
    if not body:
        return None
    try:
        return (today - date.fromisoformat(body.split()[0])).days
    except (ValueError, IndexError):
        return None


def notify(title: str, msg: str, quiet: bool) -> None:
    """Desktop notification. Best effort, never fatal: the log and the un-advanced marker
    are the channels that cannot be suppressed by a notification permission."""
    if quiet or sys.platform != "darwin":
        return
    safe = msg.replace("\\", "/").replace('"', "'")[:200]
    head = title.replace("\\", "/").replace('"', "'")[:60]
    subprocess.run(["osascript", "-e",
                    'display notification "%s" with title "%s" sound name "Basso"'
                    % (safe, head)],
                   capture_output=True)


def write_log(today: date, lines) -> Path:
    LOG_DIR.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    path = LOG_DIR / (today.isoformat() + ".log")
    with open(path, "a", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
        fh.write("\n".join(lines).rstrip() + "\n\n")
    return path


def prune(today: date) -> None:
    cutoff = today - timedelta(days=KEEP_DAYS)
    for p in LOG_DIR.glob("20*-*-*.log"):
        try:
            if date.fromisoformat(p.stem) < cutoff:
                p.unlink()
        except (ValueError, OSError):
            continue


def main() -> int:
    if "--help" in sys.argv or "-h" in sys.argv:
        print((__doc__ or "").strip())
        return 0
    force = "--force" in sys.argv
    quiet = "--quiet" in sys.argv
    if sys.platform != "darwin" and not quiet:
        print("weekly-backup: launchd and the Keychain are macOS-only; on this platform "
              "schedule with cron and set OPERATOR_OS_BACKUP_PASSPHRASE_FILE (see --help)",
              file=sys.stderr)
    today = date.today()
    started = datetime.now()
    age = marker_age(today)
    try:
        user = pwd.getpwuid(os.geteuid()).pw_name
    except KeyError:
        user = str(os.geteuid())

    head = ("=== weekly-backup %s  uid=%d user=%s  python=%s  marker=%s"
            % (started.isoformat(timespec="seconds"), os.geteuid(), user,
               sys.version.split()[0],
               "absent" if age is None else "%dd old" % age))

    if age is not None and age < BACKUP_EVERY_DAYS and not force:
        line = ("skipped: the marker is %dd old, under the %dd cadence. Nothing to do."
                % (age, BACKUP_EVERY_DAYS))
        write_log(today, [head, line])
        prune(today)
        print("weekly-backup: " + line)
        return 0

    before = marker_text()
    t0 = time.time()
    try:
        p = subprocess.run([sys.executable, str(ROOT / "scripts" / "backup-sensitive")],
                           cwd=str(ROOT), capture_output=True, text=True,
                           timeout=BACKUP_TIMEOUT_S)
        out, rc = (p.stdout + p.stderr).strip() or "(no output)", p.returncode
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        out, rc = ("TIMED OUT after %ds. No marker was written. The usual cause is a "
                   "keychain or network prompt with nobody there to answer it."
                   % BACKUP_TIMEOUT_S), TIMEOUT_RC
    except OSError as e:
        out, rc = "could not start backup-sensitive: %s" % e, SPAWN_FAIL_RC
    mins = (time.time() - t0) / 60.0

    after = marker_text()
    new_age = marker_age(date.today())
    advanced = after != before and new_age is not None and new_age <= 1

    if rc == LOCK_BUSY_RC:
        verdict = ("SKIPPED: another backup-sensitive run holds the lock. Not a failure, "
                   "and the marker was left alone.")
        exit_rc = 0
    elif rc == 0 and advanced:
        verdict = "OK: backup ran clean in %.1f min and the marker advanced." % mins
        exit_rc = 0
    elif rc == 0:
        verdict = ("FAILED: backup-sensitive exited 0 after %.1f min but the marker did not "
                   "advance. The off-site copy is NOT newer than it was." % mins)
        exit_rc = 1
    else:
        verdict = ("FAILED: backup-sensitive exited %d after %.1f min. The off-site copy did "
                   "not advance, and no marker was written." % (rc, mins))
        exit_rc = 1

    path = write_log(today, [head, verdict, "--- backup-sensitive output ---", out,
                             "--- .backup-marker now ---", after or "(absent)"])
    prune(today)

    if exit_rc != 0:
        notify("Operator OS backup FAILED", verdict.split(": ", 1)[-1], quiet)
    print("weekly-backup: %s  log: %s" % (verdict, path))
    return exit_rc


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
